1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:25,000
MethaneResponse helps analysts audit claims against traceable evidence.
It supports UNEP MARS; it does not detect methane or replace it.

2
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:50,000
This view uses a versioned MARS archive with 4,218 source records
and 30,414 plume records through 13 September 2026 UTC.

3
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Rows without a source ID remain unlinked. Source markers show archive
locations; they are not continuous measurements or methane concentration.

4
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:40,000
I selected KAZ_S_159 in Kazakhstan, an oil and gas source.
Its recorded coordinates are 43.41337° N, 52.91829° E.

5
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:56,000
MARS links three historical observations to this source.
The latest is 496 ± 226 kg/h on 11 July 2026 (EnMAP-DLR).

6
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:10,000
This is one satellite-estimated observation, not current flux.
Missing later records do not prove zero emissions or a repair.

7
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:33,000
The claim is that this release has been mitigated.
The deterministic rules baseline finds insufficient supporting evidence.

8
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The linked report text is not part of this assessment.
No live AI provider or authenticated reviewer is active in this run.

9
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:25,000
I export the audit bundle as JSON. It records the selected source,
historical dataset snapshot, claim assessment and report revision.

10
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:55,000
The verifier recomputes the local SHA-256 digest and matches it.
This report contains no blockchain receipt or on-chain transaction.

11
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Changing the report causes digest verification to fail.
That detects edits; it does not establish physical truth.

12
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:37,350
This local workflow does not prove mitigation, AI accuracy, cloud durability,
user impact, or emissions reduction. Those gates remain to be tested.
